    <Label Name="Title" Style="GalleryTitle" Text="Fibonacci Spiral" Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="16" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
    <Label Name="Description" Style="GalleryText" Text="1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13: each Fibonacci number is the sum of the two before it. Squares with these sides fit together into a rectangle — every new square is exactly as wide as the two before it side by side.\n\nA quarter circle in each square, corner to corner, makes a spiral. Every quarter turn it grows by about the same factor, close to 1.618, the golden ratio. A snail shell grows by a steady factor too, which is why it keeps the same shape as it grows.\n\nCount the spirals of seeds on a sunflower head or of scales on a pinecone: you usually get two neighbors in the Fibonacci sequence, like 8 and 13 on a pinecone or 34 and 55 on a sunflower.\n\nDrag the yellow points to turn and resize it." Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="64" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
